<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomislav of Croatia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 910–928</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimirovic-dynasty">Trpimirović dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomislav was the first ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> to be styled king, around 925. Originally Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-croatia">Dalmatian Croatia</a>, he fought off Hungarian incursions and united his land with Pannonian Croatia, and he was addressed as king in a papal letter of 925. In 926 his army defeated an invasion by the Bulgarian ruler Simeon I.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tomislav's ancestry is unknown, though he may have belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimirovic-dynasty">Trpimirović dynasty</a>, and he is generally assumed to have been a son of Muncimir. He succeeded Muncimir as Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> c. 910, the most widely accepted view, and in any case between 910 and 914; the Historia Salonitana of Thomas the Archdeacon of Split, a chronicle of the thirteenth century, names him as duke in 914. Because information on him is scarce, the exact years of his accession and death are unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Hungarians, after conquering the Carpathian Basin, raided and expanded, killing Braslav, the last Pannonian duke. The Chronicle of the Priest of Duklja credits Tomislav with a victory over them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drava">Drava</a>, though other chroniclers record Hungarian victories in the same years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> kept its northern borders and expanded into part of the collapsed Pannonian Duchy, including its former capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisak">Sisak</a>. The realm covered most of southern and central Croatia and the Dalmatian coast, excluding the Byzantine Theme of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, with parts of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and northern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. Its exact extent is disputed among historians.</p><p>By 925 Tomislav was king; the papal chancellery called him the first Croatian ruler it styled king, and a letter of Pope John X names him King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>. It is not known when, where or by whom he was crowned. In 925 he attended the Council of Split, convened to decide which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> held jurisdiction in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and to discuss Slavic liturgy.</p><p>The council gave jurisdiction to the Archbishop of Split rather than Bishop Gregory of Nin, and allowed Croatian and the Glagolitic script for local priests and monks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> was also an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and Byzantium gave Tomislav the honorary title of proconsul. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I of Bulgaria</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 924, he sent Duke Alogobotur against Croatia, and Tomislav destroyed his army in the battle of 926, probably in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Simeon's death in 927, Pope John X sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> who restored peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. How Tomislav died is unknown. He disappeared from the political scene after 928 and was succeeded by Trpimir II. Tomislav is honoured as the first Croatian king and founder of the first Croatian state. The Bosnian city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomislavgrad">Duvno</a> was renamed Tomislavgrad in 1925 for the thousandth anniversary of his coronation, and a monument to him by Robert Frangeš-Mihanović was erected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> in 1927.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomislav, King of Croatia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 925 <b>Tomislav</b> united it with the Duchy of Pannonian Croatia and is recognised as the first king, creating the Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-croatia">Duchy of Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 925 the region was united with Dalmatian Croatia under <b>Tomislav</b>, forming the Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102); the area later became Slavonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pannonian-croatia">Duchy of Pannonian Croatia</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 925 <b>Tomislav</b>, ruler of the Duchy of Croatia, also held the Duchy of Pannonian Croatia and was addressed as king in papal letters.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia (925-1102)</a></li></ul></section>
